<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:group {"align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="color:#C8102E;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;">Overview</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">What is this project?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This project showcases the basics of what can be done with a Raspberry Pi Pico, as well as real world applications, by combining MicroPython with jumper wires, leds, and resistors.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:group {"style":{"color":{"background":"#F6F6F4"},"border":{"radius":"12px"},"spacing":{"padding":{"top":"20px","bottom":"20px","left":"20px","right":"20px"}}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group" style="border-radius:12px;background-color:#F6F6F4;padding:20px">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="font-style:italic;color:#49494D">📷 Add your photo here &mdash; upload <strong>overview.png</strong> from the images folder (Overview photo)</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:separator {"opacity":"css"} -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-css-opacity"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->
<!-- wp:group {"align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="color:#C8102E;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;">Goals</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">Goals of the project</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><li>&#10003;&nbsp; Goal 1:  Use a light dependent resistor (LDR) in tangent with an RGB light, to change color as an object approaches.</li><li>&#10003;&nbsp; Goal 2: Designed to simulate a sensor giving you feedback on whether you were too close or too far.</li><li>&#10003;&nbsp; Goal 3: Utilize materials and concepts learned in Cornerstone of Engineering 1.</li><li>&#10003;&nbsp; Goal 4: Finish by the end of class and use only what’s provided in the Pico Kit.</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->
<!-- wp:group {"style":{"color":{"background":"#F6F6F4"},"border":{"radius":"12px"},"spacing":{"padding":{"top":"20px","bottom":"20px","left":"20px","right":"20px"}}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group" style="border-radius:12px;background-color:#F6F6F4;padding:20px">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="font-style:italic;color:#49494D">📷 Add your photo here &mdash; upload <strong>goals.png</strong> from the images folder (Goals photo)</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:separator {"opacity":"css"} -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-css-opacity"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">How it works</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:group {"align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="color:#C8102E;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;">How It Works</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">How It Works</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The project has the RGB LED change color based on the amount of light shown to the light dependent resistor (LDR). When nothing is covering the LDR, the light is red. As you get closer the light changes to yellow. When you’re at a sweet spot the light changes to green. When you get too close the light changes to blue. The code is written in MicroPython, aftering calibrating readings for a bright and dark environment. The current light is calculated as a proportion where 1 is dark. Then certain thresholds are set for the RGB LED to change color.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:group {"style":{"color":{"background":"#F6F6F4"},"border":{"radius":"12px"},"spacing":{"padding":{"top":"20px","bottom":"20px","left":"20px","right":"20px"}}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group" style="border-radius:12px;background-color:#F6F6F4;padding:20px">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="font-style:italic;color:#49494D">📷 Add your photo here &mdash; upload <strong>howitworks_1.png</strong> from the images folder (How It Works photo)</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:separator {"opacity":"css"} -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-css-opacity"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">How it was made</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:group {"align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="color:#C8102E;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;">Step 01</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">Step 01</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For the light dependent resistor half of the breadboard. The Pico has special pins called analog pins which reads the voltage as a range of numbers. The LDR  is connected to an analog pin. However, the Pico’s pins can only read voltage, not resistance. So the Pico and LDR alone fails to get a proper reading. To address this issue, a 10 kΩ resistor is connected to the LDR and then to ground. This creates a voltage divider, the LDR and fixed resistor split the voltage according to their resistances, so changes in light shift that split and give the Pico a voltage that rises in bright light and drops in the dark.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:group {"style":{"color":{"background":"#F6F6F4"},"border":{"radius":"12px"},"spacing":{"padding":{"top":"20px","bottom":"20px","left":"20px","right":"20px"}}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group" style="border-radius:12px;background-color:#F6F6F4;padding:20px">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="font-style:italic;color:#49494D">📷 Add your photo here &mdash; upload <strong>process_1.jpg</strong> from the images folder (Step 01 photo)</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
</div>
<!-- /wp:group -->
<!-- wp:separator {"opacity":"css"} -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-css-opacity"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->
<!-- wp:group {"align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide">
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p style="color:#C8102E;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;">Step 02</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" style="color:#1E2028">Step 02</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The RGB LED is set up on the other half of the breadboard. The RGB LED has 3 short legs and 1 long leg. The short legs are connected to the 3 different colors, red, green and blue. The long leg is meant for ground. For each of the 3 short legs connect a separate resistor with a separate pin on the PICO using jumper wires. The long leg is connected to the ground pin of the Pico.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:group {"style":{"color":{"background":"#F6F6F4"},"border":{"radius":"12px"},"spacing":{"padding":{"top":"20px","bottom":"20px","left":"20px","right":"20px"}}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
